task.openlibrary_search (completed) {
  create new command: /openlibrary search <keywords>
  example:
  https://openlibrary.org/search/inside.json?q=rust+borrow+checker

  learn from the current UI/UX of /news earch <keywords>

  in the search result, i would like to have:
  1. the book title/url/description
  2. the related text

  Implemented:
  app/chat_server.py
  - Added /openlibrary search <keywords>. It calls Open Library's full-text
  inside-search endpoint
  (https://openlibrary.org/search/inside.json?q=<keywords>) and normalizes
  each hit into a book card with title, authors, Open Library URL, catalogue
  metadata, a description, and up to three matched passages from the
  highlight snippets. Duplicate editions of the same work are merged.
  - Descriptions come from the work record when Open Library has one. They are
  fetched in parallel (cached in memory) so the search itself stays fast; when
  no catalogue description exists, a metadata description is used instead.
  - Added /openlibrary to the command list, argument completion, /help, and the
  autocomplete handler for its "search" subcommand.
  app/chat.html
  - Reused the /gutenberg book-card layout and the /news-style command result
  flow. Each card shows the title, authors, description, related full-text
  passages, and a link to the Open Library work page. Unlike /gutenberg cards,
  these cards open the source page in a new tab because the books are not
  downloaded into the local reader.
  - Added "Open Library" as the command entry label and Tab completion for
  /openlibrary search.

  Verified:
  - `search_openlibrary_inside("rust borrow checker", limit=3)` returns title,
  authors, description, Open Library URL, and related-text passages for each
  book.
  - The command emits the same `books` SSE event used by /gutenberg, and the
  usage message is shown when "search <keywords>" is missing.
  - `py_compile` passes for app/chat_server.py and `node --check` passes for the
  browser script.
}
